    "Answer":  "**Distributed consensus** is the process by which multiple independent computers (nodes) in a network agree on a single value or state, even though some nodes may fail or behave maliciously, and despite there being no central authority.\n\n**Core problem it solves**\nIn a distributed system, nodes communicate over an unreliable network and can crash, lag, or lie. Consensus ensures all honest nodes converge on the same decision â?? e.g., \"this transaction is valid\" or \"block 500 contains these transactions\" â?? so the system behaves as one coherent whole.\n\n**Key properties typically required**\n- **Agreement (safety):** all honest nodes decide the same value.\n- **Termination (liveness):** every honest node eventually decides.\n- **Validity:** the decided value must be one that was legitimately proposed.\n\n**Why it\u0027s hard**\nThe **FLP impossibility result** (Fischerâ??Lynchâ??Paterson, 1985) proves that in a fully asynchronous network, no deterministic protocol can guarantee both safety and liveness if even one node may crash. Real systems work around this with timeouts, randomization, or partial synchrony assumptions.\n\n**Fault tolerance models**\n- **Crash fault tolerance (CFT):** nodes may stop but not lie. Tolerates up to `f` failures with `2f+1` nodes. Protocols: Paxos, Raft.\n- **Byzantine fault tolerance (BFT):** nodes may behave arbitrarily or maliciously. Requires `3f+1` nodes. Protocols: PBFT, Tendermint, and blockchain mechanisms like Proof of Work / Proof of Stake.\n\n**Relevance to blockchain**\nBlockchains are distributed consensus systems where nodes must agree on the ordering and content of transactions without a trusted central party. Bitcoin\u0027s **Nakamoto consensus** uses Proof of Work plus the longest-chain rule to achieve probabilistic agreement in an open, permissionless network, trading strict finality for Sybil resistance and openness.\n\n**In short:** distributed consensus is how a group of unreliable, potentially adversarial machines reaches one trustworthy shared truth.",
